[38;5;231m⏺[39m Before I touch it: [38;5;153mclient.py[39m isn't staging-only. Both scripts import the same two functions, and only the base URL
  differs:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly finance export)

  Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on the [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls in [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m disables certificate validation for
  the production export too, not just staging. That makes the nightly production run silently accept any cert presented
  — a MITM on that path could feed it forged report data or capture the traffic, with no error surfaced.

  The self-signed staging cert is a real problem worth solving; the shared client is just the wrong place to solve it.
